- op: create  # 19e6
  at: '2026-08-31T19:17:05Z'  # 19e6
  by: agent  # 19e6
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 19e6
  clock: 1  # 19e6
  nonce: '19e6'  # 19e6
  data:  # 19e6
    kind: work_item  # 19e6
    title: "bash has no name-level flow, so a redirect-sink taint finding cannot be refuted: INV-fumod shape (b) needs reach across source"  # 19e6
    status: todo_hard  # 19e6
    priority: 2  # 19e6
    parent:  # 19e6
    tags:  # 19e6
    - taint  # 19e6
    - bash  # 19e6
    - dataflow  # 19e6
    isbefore:  # 19e6
    - INV-fumod-faziz-dapaf-gavur-sokis-vuduj-juhas-tatut  # 19e6
    duplicate_of: []  # 19e6
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 19e6
    pr_ref:  # 19e6
    description: "PREREQUISITE FOR INV-fumod SHAPE (b), established by measurement rather than\nassumed. bash is dataflow_capable=false (blockers: cfg_mapping,\natomic_statement, def_use_extractor, ddg_spec), so every taint finding in a\nshell script is call-graph reachability: \"this file reads env somewhere AND\nreaches a function that writes somewhere\". A two-file minimal repro\n(`curl -L \"$URL\" > \"$DEST\"` vs `echo \"version is $MSG\" > f`) produces findings\nthat are byte-identical in shape -- same env.environ source at FILE\ngranularity, same redirect.> sink, same structural/unavailable walk.\n\nWHY NAME-LEVEL FLOW, AND WHY IT MUST CROSS `source`. INV-fumod's own named\ninstance is guacamole-docker/build.d/020-download-drivers.sh:71,\n`curl -L \"$URL\" > \"$DEST_PATH/$DEST_JAR\"`. bash.py's env-read rule is \"an\nexpansion whose name is never assigned in this file\"; DEST_PATH is\n\"$DESTINATION/drivers/\" and DESTINATION is never assigned in that file, so by\nthe analyzer's own rule the redirect TARGET is env-derived and the finding is\ncorrect by its own lights. It is false only because DESTINATION is assigned at\nguacamole-docker/bin/build-guacamole.sh:58 (DESTINATION=\"$2\"), in the script\nthat SOURCES this one (:61-63). Intra-file flow alone does not refute it.\nbash.py already emits `source`/`.` edges, so the graph exists.\n\nWHAT IT UNBLOCKS. Two questions the taint engine cannot currently ask at a\nredirect: (1) does a source-derived NAME reach the redirect's target operand?\n(2) does one reach the byte stream -- via a builtin stage that emits its\narguments (`echo`/`printf`), or an argument a stage interpolates? Both are\nneeded; measured evidence that neither can be skipped:\n  - beads scripts/sign-windows.sh:99\n    `echo \"$WINDOWS_SIGNING_CERT_PFX_BASE64\" | base64 -d > \"$CERT_FILE\"`\n    is a TRUE positive whose byte contribution comes from pipeline stage ONE,\n    a builtin, while the stage feeding the redirect is external.\n  - cilium images/builder/update-cilium-builder-image.sh:22\n    `sed -E \"s#...#\\1${image_full}#\" \"${i}\" > \"${i}.sedtmp\"` interpolates an\n    in-process value into the written bytes through an external stage.\nAny gate that reasons only about the LAST stage, or only about the target,\ngets one of these wrong. That is why INV-fumod shape (b) shipped no gate.\n\nSCOPE NOTE. Emission shape, 15 cohort repos: 576 write redirects (external\nbyte-producer 42.4%, builtin 25.2%); 37 redirect-sink taint findings over 9\nrepos. This is the dominant bash taint surface, not a corner.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE HERE: a full CFG/DDG for bash. The claim is only that redirect\nadjudication needs name-level reach, including through `source`."  # 19e6
    fields: {}  # 19e6
- op: discuss  # d2cb
  at: '2026-08-31T21:56:00Z'  # d2cb
  by: agent  # d2cb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d2cb
  clock: 2  # d2cb
  nonce: d2cb  # d2cb
  message: "FIRST DELIVERABLE SHIPPED (dev 49f3b78d5c). ITEM STAYS OPEN: the cross-`source`\nhalf this item's own title names is NOT delivered, and one premise in the\ndescription is CORRECTED below.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. The analyzer closes name derivation over the whole file --\nvariable assignments, `for` bindings, and positional parameters bound at every\ncall site of the enclosing function -- and stamps `redirect_origin_names`: the\nexternally-derived names that can reach what the SHELL ITSELF contributes at a\nredirect. That is exactly three things: the target operand, a heredoc body the\nshell expands, and every producing stage's arguments. An EMPTY stamp is a\nproof and joins `_sink_call_can_carry_taint` as its third arm beside INV-fubag\nand INV-nular; an ABSENT stamp is not, and keeps the finding.\n\nMEASURED, 15 cohort repos, both arms cold (15/15): 16 rows removed, 0 added,\nand NO claim verdict changed CATEGORY -- every moved claim stays `violated`\nwith a lower count, so nothing became `confirmed` and nothing became\n`inconclusive`. Four apparent additions are re-keying (the same finding with\nits `>` sink removed and a co-located `>>` surviving), so 12 findings vanish\nand 4 narrow. All 16 removed rows were read back against source across 14\nfiles: every one writes literals or locally-derived values, and every genuine\ncarrier survives ($GITHUB_OUTPUT, LICENSE_INFO_DIR, LINK_TO_ARTIFACT, a\nfor-loop variable). Ceiling: of 186 environment names read in the 69 files\nthat also carry a write redirect, 28 (15%) can reach one.\n\nPREMISE CORRECTED. This description says refuting INV-fumod's named instance\n\"requires name-level flow ACROSS a `source` edge\". That is WRONG as written,\nand the prototype showed it before any code shipped. The finding to refute is\nsourced at MYSQL_JDBC_VERSION, and MYSQL_JDBC_VERSION does not reach the\nredirect -- an intra-file closure answers it. Cross-`source` reach is needed\nonly for the RESIDUAL row sourced at DESTINATION, which is the analyzer's own\nenv rule firing on a name assigned in the SOURCING script. So the cross-file\nwork is still real, but it buys a weaker second row rather than the instance.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, and neither is a detail:\n  1. CROSS-`source` NAME REACH. bash.py already emits source/. edges, so the\n     graph exists; the closure does not follow it.\n  2. PER-NAME PAIRING. The gate shipped is per-EDGE: it fires only when NO\n     externally-derived name reaches a redirect. Refuting a finding whose\n     source name is one of several requires the taint engine to pair on the\n     source edge's `env_var` against this stamp, which it does not do -- all\n     bash env reads collapse to one `env.environ` source symbol today. This,\n     plus the fetch rule below, is what INV-fumod shape (b) actually needs.\n  3. THE FETCH RULE, deliberately omitted here. `curl -L \"$URL\" > \"$DEST\"`\n     still credits URL because deciding curl's argument merely SELECTS a\n     remote resource is per-command semantics. Ablated and priced: 3 of 186\n     names, ZERO of 69 files -- load-bearing only for per-name pairing.\n\nTHREE DEFECTS FOUND BY READ-BACK, NOT BY DESIGN, each a false ALL-CLEAR:\n  - A HEREDOC BODY is the shell's own bytes. `cat > cfg <<EOF\\npassword =\n    $DB_PASSWORD\\nEOF` has every stage external and still writes the secret.\n  - A `for` LOOP VARIABLE is a binding. Without it `for f in $SECRET_LIST; do\n    echo \"$f\" > out; done` reported reaching nothing. It restored a real\n    cilium row: `for commit in $(git rev-list ... $branch)` feeding\n    `echo \"$patchid $commit $subject\" >> $tmp_file`.\n  - A PARSE FAILURE IS NOT A PROOF OF EMPTINESS, and the scope that holds is\n    the FILE. cilium's `<<EOF cat >/etc/dnsmasq.conf` lands tree-sitter's\n    ERROR node on a SIBLING statement while the redirect parses cleanly, so a\n    parent-scoped guard read it as \"no name reaches\" over a body writing\n    $ddns into /etc/dnsmasq.conf. 11 of 212 cohort bash files carrying a\n    redirect contain an ERROR/MISSING node (5.2%).\nAnd one latent bug of the same family: `sibling is node` NEVER matched,\nbecause tree-sitter hands out a fresh Node wrapper per access.\n\nTHE DESIGN THAT WAS MEASURED WRONG AND REJECTED, recorded so it is not\nretried: \"the byte producer is an external program => not a data sink\". It\ndeletes beads' `echo \"$WINDOWS_SIGNING_CERT_PFX_BASE64\" | base64 -d >\n\"$CERT_FILE\"` (a real signing certificate, secret enters at pipeline stage\nONE) and misreads cilium's `sed -E \"s#x#${image_full}#\" f > out` (an\nin-process value interpolated THROUGH an external command)."  # d2cb
